WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.710
- Hi everyone. Good evening.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.270
My name is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.510
I am an astrophysicist
and the current director

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.320
of Yale's Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.890
I am delighted to have
you all, to welcome you

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:24.180
to today's wonderful talk
titled White Gold Fever,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.590
The Story of Deep Sea Treasure
and an Environmental Tragedy

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.013
by the Esteemed Journalist Esther Honig.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.500
Before I start giving you
a brief bio of Esther's,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.760
I would like to recognize our benefactors,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.850
Mr. And Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.030
whose support for interdisciplinary
scholarship at Yale

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.213
has made this and many other
ventures around Yale possible.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.750
In particular, I give out a warm shout out

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.990
to Barbara Franke, who
is in the audience today.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.810
And as part of my required duties,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.650
let me also remind you that

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.310
for those of us who are assembled,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.070
that we are recording this
event and that all participants

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.930
will therefore have to
have their videos muted

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.380
for the duration of the talk.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.110
Questions may be submitted at
any time to the Q&amp;A feature,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.800
and we will have dedicated Q&amp;A session

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.040
after Esther's talk.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.840
So let me tell you a little bit more

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.230
about this fascinating author.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:32.760
Esther works both in print
and audio to create stories

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.820
that are at the focus of the
intersection of agriculture,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.670
immigration, and climate change.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.210
A perfect fit to the
interdisciplinary nature

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.870
of the activities at Frankie.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.460
She's a fluent Spanish speaker

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.950
with a degree in Spanish
and Latin American studies,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.860
and she travels to
Mexico and across the US

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.840
to carry out her reporting.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.970
She's an incredible storyteller,

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.950
as you'll all see very shortly.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.490
Her work has been published
by The Nation, Snap Judgment,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.910
Latino USA, National Public
Radio, and much more.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.710
Her piece for Mother Jones Magazine

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.750
about the spread of COVID-19

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.780
inside Midwestern meat
packing plants was cited

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.100
by US Senators in a letter
confronting corporate executives.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.790
So she also not only tells stories,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:24.870
but stories that make us
understand accountability.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.470
Accountability to each other,
accountability to the earth,

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.070
accountability to climate change.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.860
She's the recipient of the
UC Berkeley's 11th Hour

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.210
Food and Farming Fellowship,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.680
and her work has been recognized

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.780
by the Edward R Murrow Award

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.270
and the Society for
Professional Journalists.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.160
She has been invited to present her work

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.950
at tons of conferences and universities.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.450
She cut her teeth as a reporter

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.970
working for public radio
stations across the country

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.210
where she covered everything
from the opioid epidemic

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:57.060
to impacts of Trump's
immigration policies.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.360
These days, Esther noted
that she prefers to work on

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.140
in depth narrative driven
stories that stray as far away

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.140
as possible from the grind
of the immediate daily news.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.680
So before I turn over to
Esther for this evening,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.250
let me just mention our
Learning by Doing Project.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.220
Please consider contributing
simple science experiments

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.740
that you can do at home for kids

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.020
to our website, little videos.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.570
Take a look at our website
to see how to do that.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.500
And I also want to advertise
our event as part of the

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.500
Understanding the Nature of
Inference colloquium series.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.710
We have our virtual talk by
Professor Brian Cantwell-Smith,

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.260
which is on Wednesday, December 7th,

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.800
and a discussion session on
the eighth, both at 3:00 PM.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.270
Anyway, without further ado,

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.510
I would like to turn the
floor over to Esther Honig.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.370
Thank you so much, Esther, for coming.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.493
We're absolutely delighted and
cannot wait to hear from you.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.050
- Thank you so much. That was
such a lovely introduction.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.790
I'm so excited to be here today

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.220
and share this story with you.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.930
This took many months.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.660
It was part of a strong team
of editors and producers

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.590
at Snap Judgment.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.050
And without further ado,

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.820
we're just gonna jump into
it and I'm gonna share

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.760
this story that I love
so much with you all.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.010
So I'm gonna share my screen.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.560
All right, White Gold Fever,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.743
Story of Deep Sea Treasure
and Environmental Tragedy.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.680
So our story begins,
this is Belen Delgado,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.230
and he's the protagonist of our story.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.450
He first learned to fish at the age of 10,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.210
and he's been doing this all his life.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.040
And I'm just gonna
start off by playing you

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.883
the opening cut of tape from my story.

00:04:42.243 --> 00:04:45.035
(waves crashing)

00:04:45.035 --> 00:04:50.035
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:04:55.013 --> 00:05:00.013
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:09.908
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:05:09.908 --> 00:05:12.408
(waves crash)

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:16.560
- [Esther] Standing on an
empty beach covered in fog,

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.230
Belen Delgado points to
an area on the horizon

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.360
where 15 years ago he found something

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.800
that would change his life

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.500
and the lives of everyone he knew.

00:05:27.528 --> 00:05:29.880
- [Translator] Well, the truth is,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.210
it was one of the best chapters of my life

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.853
for my family, for my town.

00:05:37.109 --> 00:05:40.966
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:05:40.966 --> 00:05:43.716
(waves crashing)

00:05:45.476 --> 00:05:46.920
- All right, I hope that helped everyone

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.080
get a little transported into this story.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.170
That's why I love working
with audio so much.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.953
So this is Teacapan, we
have right here on the map,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:00.060
and that's where our story
takes place 15 years ago.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.090
This is a fishing town
of about 4,000 people

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.740
located in Sinaloa, Mexico

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.200
at the most southern point of the state.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.830
It is such a small town, there's
not a single traffic light.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.170
There's no grocery store.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:16.890
There's one or two paved
roads in the entire town.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.470
So this is a fishing town.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.540
Even on the entrance to the town,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.730
there's a big archway and it says,

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.073
Teacapan, Town of Fishermen.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.130
Every day at the beach,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.380
there are fishermen arriving from their,

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.430
every morning at the beach,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.950
there are fishermen
arriving with their catch

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.360
from the night before.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.410
They're fishing mackerel,
sea bass, stingray, shark.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.180
They even used to fish sea turtle

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.040
back when it was still legal.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.060
And everything here, the
culture, the daily life,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.183
the traditions, it all
circulates around fishing.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.090
So this again, is a catch

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.400
that was brought in from the ocean.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.740
Fishermen spend the
whole night working out

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.733
on the open ocean catching
fish to bring into the market.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.830
This man right here is
holding mackerel, or sierra,

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.740
and this is the preferred
fish for making ceviche.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.870
Sinaloa is known for its ceviche,

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.640
but this town, it is seafood and ceviche.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.400
I mean, it's at every
party, for breakfast,

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:24.980
you eat fried fish.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.380
It is, seafood and fish is your breakfast,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.940
lunch, and dinner.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.000
And even the food itself,

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.280
the way that they prepare
the ceviche's unlike anything

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.013
that I've seen in other
parts of the state.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.890
Like again, just to show you
that it's just such a big part

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.240
of this town.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.920
You'll be walking down the street

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.200
and you'll run into
someone like this fisherman

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.733
who's repairing his net.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.573
It's just everywhere that you look.

00:07:57.954 --> 00:08:00.480
This is a shrine for St. Andrew.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.450
He's the patron saint of fisherman.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.310
And the shrine is right,

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:08.190
it's overlooking the dock
where fishermen leave at dusk

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.780
to spend the night out on the ocean

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.953
so they can stop here first
and pray for protection.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.120
So our story begins here in this town,

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:21.660
and it's 15 years ago,

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:24.480
and they're going through
a particularly bad spell.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.060
There's been rampant
over fishing in the area,

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.990
and suddenly the populations
of shrimp, of fish

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.090
are starting to drop off.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.240
Many of the fishermen are
struggling to make a living,

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.320
keep food on the table.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.170
There are families who are growing hungry,

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:41.100
who are going hungry,

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.620
and no one really knows
what's happened to the fish.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.873
They just know that they're gone.

00:08:47.155 --> 00:08:49.110
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.067
- [Translator] We'd
send people out to fish

00:08:50.067 --> 00:08:52.140
and they wouldn't bring back anything.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.930
In fact, many would say, what's going on?

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:58.830
And it's like, well, who
knows? There aren't any fish.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:08:59.763
There's nothing.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.000
- It's getting so bad, the
local businesses are closing.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.400
People are even trying to
sell their fishing boats.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.890
But of course no one wants to buy them.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:13.350
And so one evening, Belen is sitting out

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.570
in front of his house with his old friend,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.190
he's also a fisherman.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.650
And they're talking
after a long day at work.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.390
And that's when Belen decides

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:25.413
he's gonna share a bit of
gossip that he heard that day.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.400
And it was from a guy who
works on the shrimping boats.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.390
Now, these are bigger boats
and they have large nets,

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:36.000
trawling nets that they
drop into the ocean,

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.540
they scrape along the ocean
floor and they bring up shrimp.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:41.130
And earlier that day,

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.440
this man had been working
on the shrimping boat.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.450
They dropped their nets
in like they always do.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.150
They pull them back up and
when they pull up the net,

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.820
they discovered something
caught in the webbing

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.193
that was beyond shocking.

00:09:54.648 --> 00:09:56.970
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.595
- [Translator] One, and then another

00:09:58.595 --> 00:10:01.316
callo de hacha fell out.

00:10:01.316 --> 00:10:04.498
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:10:04.498 --> 00:10:07.740
El callo, it's one of those species that,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.240
let's put it this way,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.353
it's one of the most expensive
things you can catch.

00:10:12.353 --> 00:10:14.190
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.570
- All right, this is the callo de hacha.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.840
callo means scallop, and
hacha translates to hatchet,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.960
and it gets that name for
the shape of its shell,

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.360
which looks like a hatchet.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.970
So over here you have one that is open,

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:32.010
you have the entrails,

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.950
and then right here you have
this coin of white meat.

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:38.460
And that is the part that's consumed.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.320
That usually eaten raw,

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.380
and it is considered a delicacy.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.650
I also understand that it's
native to the Sea of Cortez,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.170
and this is a product
that, it's a delicacy.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.480
It's sold to high end restaurants

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:54.660
all along the coast in Mexico.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.940
It's also shipped out to Mexico City

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.020
where it's sold, again, in
very upscale restaurants.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.250
And as Belen reiterates,

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.650
this is one of the most expensive things

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.203
you can possibly find in their oceans.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.370
So no one had ever
caught this type of callo

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:12.360
in Teacapan before.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.100
And they're a little bit
shocked by this story

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:21.180
that a callo has appeared
in a shrimping net.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.520
Then the following week when
a shrimping boat catches

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.910
another callo, they bring it to Belen,

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.910
and that's when he
starts to get a big idea.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:33.150
Belen, who went to college
for fisheries biology,

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:35.640
he knows that this isn't
just a coincidence.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:38.100
Typically, callo, were
jammed between rocks.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.420
And so when you harvest them,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.940
you have to kind of cut them out,

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:43.620
de-lodge them, if you will,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.100
from the crevasse where
they've been living.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.090
So the mere fact that they've
been swept up in this net

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.640
tells Belen that there are so many of them

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.710
piled on the ocean floor,

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:59.580
that they can be easily collected

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:04.580
or cut loose by the gentle
tug of one of these nets.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.850
So he's starting to imagine
that there is a pile of callo

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.923
lurking beneath the ocean water.

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:18.110
And that's when he starts to make a plan.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:23.100
So here, Teacapan, from
the satellite imagery.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:26.670
Here, this snaking river is the estuary,

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:31.500
and that's what lent this town
to being a town of fishermen.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.880
That's what it is founded for,

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.670
is because it was such a
great location for fishing.

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.730
And it is in large part
because of this estuary.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:43.380
So right here off of the
mouth of the estuary,

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:46.170
you have Playa La Tambora.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:50.100
So that is, it's right around here

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.530
that this shrimping boat is going down

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:54.453
and catching these callo and it's net.

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:58.010
And so Belen decides,
that what they need to do

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.820
is they have to get a scuba diver.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:03.540
Because this is deeper
than any of them can go.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.970
It's about 80 feet below the surface.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:10.950
No one had the equipment or
the knowhow to dive that far.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:14.130
So he goes to the local, a
nearby city of Escuinapa,

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:17.520
about 45 minutes away,
they don't find anything.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.970
They drive two hours away to
the larger city of Mazatlan

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:23.010
and they still, they
can't find the equipment,

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.830
they can't find anyone
who's able to do this.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.540
And then finally, Belen calls up a friend

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.630
in Baja, California, we're
diving is more common.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.460
Fishermen make a living this way

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:36.690
by going down into the ocean
and harvesting big mollusks

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.610
and things like callo.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:43.050
And he gets a guy, a diver, on the phone

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:46.470
and he gets him to agree to come down.

00:13:47.604 --> 00:13:49.470
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.690
- [Translator] We asked him,
how much will you charge us

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:52.830
to do this job?

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:55.710
And he said, how about we make a deal?

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:59.010
If there's callo I won't
charge you for the trip,

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:01.830
but you'll give me a
chance to fish some of it.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:05.490
If there's nothing, you'll pay
for my trip there and back.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:06.690
- [Belen] Okay, Esta bien.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.208
- [Translator] So he came.

00:14:08.208 --> 00:14:11.504
- [Esther] But also wanted
to keep this a secret.

00:14:11.504 --> 00:14:13.620
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.360
- [Translator] Let's be discreet

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.180
so that this doesn't
turn into complete chaos.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.360
Everything must be done carefully.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.113
I said, you know what?

00:14:23.113 --> 00:14:26.412
If there are callo, don't tell anyone.

00:14:26.412 --> 00:14:28.031
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:14:28.031 --> 00:14:31.020
- Okay? So they have a bit
of an agreement going on.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:35.040
The diver comes down, they
take him out to an area,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.990
more or less around here.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.810
They get his equipment
on the boat, they go out,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.080
he puts on his white fisherman boots

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.360
and he revs up his air compressor.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.720
So no one is diving
here with an oxygen tank

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.310
because that's far too expensive.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.170
And I'm gonna show you
what that looks like

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:53.283
in just a minute.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:57.780
Using an air compressor is
the preferred means of diving,

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:59.970
and it's also very dangerous.

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:01.920
So the diver puts on a lead belt,

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:03.720
which helps him sink to the bottom.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:06.870
He flops over the side of the boat

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:10.740
and he sinks to the bottom of the ocean.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.400
And about an hour passes,
Belen, his friend,

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.950
his brother-in-law, they're
all on the boat just waiting

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.473
to see what he returns with.

00:15:21.212 --> 00:15:23.220
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:25.110
- [Translator] And when the diver came up,

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:30.053
the surprise he had, 100 pounds of callo.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.903
I said to him, what did
it look like down there?

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:38.640
You all are sitting on a mine of gold.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:42.443
He says, wherever you step, there's callo.

00:15:43.311 --> 00:15:46.140
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.643
- [Translator] Oh, dear God.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.320
- So the men had assembled
upon a massive colony

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.480
of shellfish just off the town shore.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.730
And this is equivalent to finding

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.800
something like sunken treasure.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.110
Researchers would later
determine that it covered

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:02.310
a stretch of ocean floor,

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:06.750
about 35 miles long and a mile wide,

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:08.610
looked something like this.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:12.153
And the estimated value was
somewhere around $2 million.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:17.280
So the next day, Belen gets in his truck

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:20.160
and he drives two hours away to Mazatlan

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.430
to go to this big government building

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:26.310
where the federal fishing
authorities are housed.

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:27.543
It's called Conapesca.

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:30.180
And he gets a meeting
with one of the higher ups

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.700
and he tries to explain his situation.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.820
To prove what he's found, he has a cooler

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.790
full of his shellfish, of the callo.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:42.180
'Cause he needs them to believe him.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:45.480
And he explains what he wants.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.540
He wants there to be permits
for all the fishermen

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:52.350
in his town so that callo can
be caught and sold legally.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.760
And he also knows

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.040
that there needs to be
some sort of regulation.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.170
They need to have a quota system

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:01.260
because if there isn't
some sort of intervention,

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:04.380
this is gonna be immediately over-fished.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:08.310
He is aware of just how desperate
the men in his town are,

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:11.100
because remember, they're
coming off of the last few years

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:13.590
of very, very bad fishing levels.

00:17:13.590 --> 00:17:18.590
So this is important that
it be controlled somehow.

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:24.890
And he's basically begging
this agent to come in and help.

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.640
And the agent says, yeah, you know,

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:28.230
we'll see what we can do.

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:31.440
Belen leaves feeling like,
okay, I've done the right thing,

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:32.890
I've gotten the ball rolling.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.930
Meanwhile, back in Teacapan,

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:40.150
unfortunately the scuba diver from Baja

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.130
is out there catching callo

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.770
because that was the
agreement that they had.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:51.030
And the people in Teacapan
can see him from the shore.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.440
It's so close.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:56.760
And they see this guy, a
scuba diver, diving in,

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.130
coming up a little bit while later.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:00.933
And they get curious.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.420
There's always, Belen says,

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:05.910
there's always a lot of
gossip between the fishermen.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:07.680
So someone eventually gets in their boat

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:12.680
and they scuttle over
to see what is going on.

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:16.852
And they see all of these
big bags of callo de hacha

00:18:16.852 --> 00:18:20.310
sitting in the boat and it's
like a bomb goes off, right?

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:21.810
This guy goes back to town,

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:24.390
he tells everyone what he's just seen,

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:27.423
word is out and mayhem ensues.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.560
To quote myself from my story,

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:33.900
almost immediately, everyone in the town,

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.840
some of whom had never
fished before in their lives,

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.910
teachers, politicians, ranchers,

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:41.520
bought boats and hired divers.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.110
And men who didn't know how to dive

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:44.820
were signing up for the job.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:48.063
There was money to be made
and everyone wanted in.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.503
Oh, sorry.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:56.663
Nope.

00:18:56.663 --> 00:18:59.613
I might be missing a piece
of audio. That's okay.

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:01.953
Yes. Okay,

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:07.920
So there's this piece of
audio that I wanted to share,

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:11.373
and the Belen is, I'll
just read it for you.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:15.930
There was a doctor, a good
friend of the fisherman,

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:17.490
he bought three boats.

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:19.470
He didn't even need the money.

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:23.010
It made me so mad, so angry
to see this type of person

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.280
take advantage and they
could give a damn, right?

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:29.130
So this piece of audio really, I feel,

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:33.000
illustrates this question that
we're starting to confront,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.060
which is who does this belong to?

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:38.040
In Belen's mind and the fisherman's mind,

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:40.590
they've dedicated their
whole lives to this trade.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.060
And now that there's this opportunity

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:43.380
to make a lot of money,

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:46.020
they fully believe that
this is rightfully theirs.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.910
Of course, this is a natural resource.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:50.520
There is no intervention from
authorities at this point.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:54.180
No one is saying who can
and cannot harvest callo.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:58.800
And so people are being
opportunistic and is often the case.

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:00.303
The rich are getting richer.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:05.730
Now, one positive aspect to
the discovery of the callo,

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:07.460
of everyone going out and fishing callo,

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:11.400
is that very suddenly there's
a ton of money in this town.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.840
It is exploding with earnings.

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:20.340
People who are just
making $10 before, a day,

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:23.790
are now making a hundred to even $150.

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:27.150
That is the price that this
shellfish is earning them

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:28.590
on the market.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.570
In a town where shops are closing,

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.690
now they're living in this moment of glory

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:36.630
and things are being revived,
shops are being reopened.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:41.630
Everyone is looking for any
excuse they can to party.

00:20:41.737 --> 00:20:43.230
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.480
- [Translator] Let's just
say there was a lot of money.

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:48.930
People bought cars and as
much beer as they wanted,

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:51.150
they had big weddings and parties.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:52.800
If they saw you walking by,

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.300
they'd just offer you a
plate of callo de hacha

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:58.620
which is something that we never ate.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:01.077
And now we could eat all we wanted.

00:21:01.077 --> 00:21:03.720
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.040
We felt proud.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:08.190
We felt really important
because during this time,

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:13.190
the economy of the Teacapan,
I tell you, grew by 1000%.

00:21:13.905 --> 00:21:16.260
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:18.840
- [Esther] Belen Started fishing too.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:22.530
Now, something that I
thought was really important

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:26.340
while writing this story is
that had it been told from the,

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.700
had it not been told from the
perspective of the fishermen,

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.640
like in any sort of traditional article,

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:36.450
they might have described
the fishermen as the villains

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.000
and the callo as the prey.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:42.750
And what I wanted to do was
show that these are families,

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.920
these are human beings and their parents

00:21:46.756 --> 00:21:50.730
and for that matter,
they're, like most of us,

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:53.373
interested in improving their lot in life.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:56.700
Yes, there were parties
and there were bad actors

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:59.100
who were exploiting the situation,

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:01.830
but because of this money,

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:05.190
people are able to do things
they could have never imagined.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:07.950
Like put their children
through college or buy a car

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:09.870
that they could later
use to start a business

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:12.660
or install running water in their homes.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:16.080
And so I often think when
we observe these situations,

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:20.040
things like poaching or the exploitation

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:21.690
of natural resources,

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.580
and we're standing at this position

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:25.170
of immense economic privilege.

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:28.260
And I say that as someone who
lives in the United States,

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:31.480
and we have a tendency to
sympathize with the animals

00:22:33.702 --> 00:22:36.510
that are being extracted or hunted.

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:39.270
And I wanted to show that
I believe these communities

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.823
of people also deserve our empathy.

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:46.440
These are people, many of
whom are living hand to mouth,

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:49.710
truly on in many cases
the brink of survival.

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:54.710
And so yes, we may see them as greedy,

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:57.480
but it is not entirely
that black and white.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:58.833
There is nuance here.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:06.450
So a few weeks go by since
the callo have been discovered

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.220
and the news has spread.

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.590
People are coming from across
the state to fish callo.

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:13.980
And then the news catches on
and reporters have come to town

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:17.280
and they love this story
of like the Cinderella town

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:20.760
that was down on their luck
and now suddenly is exploding

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.920
with opportunity and money.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.760
The headlines are things
like the Cocaine of the Sea

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.080
is what they're calling it.

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:30.780
Another one is of course white gold fever,

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:33.270
which became the title for this piece.

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:36.150
And I found this clip from Mundo Vision

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:38.490
and they did like a long segment on it.

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:42.180
This was not just local
news, this was national.

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:44.700
When it first broke, this was a big deal

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:47.070
and people were really
excited and interested

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:48.573
in seeing what was happening.

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:52.590
So I just wanted to show this to you

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:56.463
because we can actually
see the folks diving.

00:23:59.802 --> 00:24:02.302
(light music)

00:24:03.322 --> 00:24:05.910
Now, this is entirely in Spanish,

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:07.980
so I'm just gonna narrate a little bit.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:10.530
I just wanted to show people a little bit

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.090
of what we're seeing just in terms of

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:13.890
like the talent itself.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:18.180
These are guys getting ready
to go fishing for the day

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:20.340
to go catch some callo.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.350
This of course is the
fisherman that the reporter

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.020
is shadowing and he's explaining,

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:28.890
they're all gonna drive up about
or boat up about 25 minutes

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:33.890
to where this giant bank of
callo has been discovered.

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:37.110
I'm gonna fast forward a little bit, okay,

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:40.353
here we're gonna see the diving.

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:44.433
Okay, so as I mentioned,

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:50.370
we do not use, this is not a oxygen tank.

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:51.930
That tube right there

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:53.760
is what's connected to the air compressor

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:56.280
that is forcing oxygen through that tube

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.380
that then goes into the divers mask.

00:24:58.380 --> 00:24:59.580
That is an air compressor

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:01.350
that's run off the gasoline, right?

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:02.700
This is pretty rudimentary.

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:05.490
Here we have our diver
and he's talking about

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:07.350
some of the dangers that are inherent

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:09.060
with using this machine.

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:12.840
The tube can get tied up,
sometimes it can break.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.483
If the machine breaks while
the divers under water,

00:25:15.483 --> 00:25:17.790
they have to be careful, they
can't come up too quickly.

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.890
They'll get the bends, very dangerous.

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:21.914
See that's his lead belt.

00:25:21.914 --> 00:25:26.370
And you see that he's wearing
jeans and fishing boots.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:27.630
And I think this part is so cool

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:30.990
because the cameramen
actually went down with him

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:33.590
or they brought a camera
down there so we could see.

00:25:35.967 --> 00:25:38.367
Now you'll see he's holding
a rope in his hands.

00:25:39.396 --> 00:25:41.613
And that is el cabo de vita.

00:25:42.795 --> 00:25:46.020
So the fishermen who are
waiting up top, in that boat,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:46.853
you see it?

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:50.730
They're waiting to see if they feel a tug.

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:52.140
If he feels two tugs,

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:54.240
that means that the dive
is ready to come back up.

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:57.150
And they'll pull that rope and
they'll deliver him back up.

00:25:57.150 --> 00:26:00.543
If it's one tug, there's
something that's gone wrong,

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.600
something has happened on the ocean floor

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:05.190
and he needs help now.

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:08.580
And that is the extent
of their communication.

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:13.580
So as you can imagine,
incredibly risky, very dangerous.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:17.550
And this is how all of this
callo is being extracted,

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:19.590
not just at this moment Teacapan,

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:23.343
but this is pretty widespread
across all of Mexico.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:29.520
So it's been okay.

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:32.940
It's been about four months
since the callo were found.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:35.280
And now the amount of boats
that are out on the water

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:36.750
has more than tripled.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:40.080
There are about 300 boats
out there on a daily basis,

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:43.530
and they're all harvesting
around a hundred to 200 pounds

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:45.270
a day of pure callo meat.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.820
So not even counting the shells.

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.100
And this entire time
Belen has been fishing

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:51.930
under a self-imposed quota.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:54.270
He thinks that he needs to set an example

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:55.230
for the other fishermen

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:58.290
because this is the
responsible thing to do.

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:00.390
He owns four fishing boats

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:02.610
and he has fishermen who
go out and fish for him.

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:04.353
And he is only gonna send in two.

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:10.260
And this is his way of
sort of demonstrating

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:12.750
that we can control ourselves.

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:17.750
We don't have to fish
this into extinction.

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:20.607
So at around that time,
a local university comes

00:27:20.607 --> 00:27:23.760
and they perform a study
of the callo population.

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:26.220
They hold a meeting for
all of the fishermen

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.290
out on the boardwalk,

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:32.880
and they tell them that this
bank of callo is so large,

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:34.950
it can last 10 years.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:37.290
Other sources claim it
could have lasted as long

00:27:37.290 --> 00:27:41.280
as 30 years, but they have
to fish it sustainably.

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:43.170
That means limiting how much is harvested,

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:45.810
so that the population it can reproduce.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:48.840
And this is Belen, he feel like,

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:50.640
he feels like he's found his moment

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:53.763
and he stands up and he talks to everyone.

00:27:55.699 --> 00:27:57.270
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:59.340
- [Translator] Gentleman,
we have to take care

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:00.720
of the shellfish.

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:03.573
We have to ration how much we catch.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:08.340
What do we gain by
fishing 200 pounds each?

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.550
If they're not going to
give us a good price,

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:15.180
let's only fish 100 pounds per boat,

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:16.980
so they pay us a higher price

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:19.916
and the callo will last longer.

00:28:19.916 --> 00:28:21.060
(Belen speaking Spanish)

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:23.790
- So understands they
cannot keep extracting callo

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:25.050
at the current rate.

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:26.970
And in no time at all,
they're gonna be back

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.970
to where they were before
with nothing left to fish.

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:32.937
He also knows that they're
flooding the market with callo,

00:28:32.937 --> 00:28:34.710
and that's driving the price down.

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:36.450
It's now at about half of what it was

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:38.610
when they originally started out.

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:42.390
And he wants everyone
to abide by this quota.

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:43.830
And he knows it can work

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:46.380
because in other communities in Mexico,

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:49.260
fishermen preserve the fish populations

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:52.650
by fishing under these
self-imposed quotas.

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:56.340
He knows it can work, but the fishermen,

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:58.023
they don't seem to believe him.

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:02.040
Perhaps they don't believe that
the callo will ever run out.

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:05.640
Maybe they think, maybe they're so happy

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:10.050
that they don't wanna admit
that this euphoric chapter

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:12.510
of fishing will eventually end.

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:14.460
And I think in many ways
it also speaks as something

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:17.910
that Belen shares, which is
the mentality of the fishermen.

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:19.500
And that is that you have to fish today

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:21.060
because you don't know
if you're gonna be able

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:23.130
to fish tomorrow.

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.410
So this sort of survival mentality,

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:29.070
short term thinking is
really what he's up against

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:34.070
in trying to convince them
to change their approach.

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:37.530
So after about six months,

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.200
the authorities have finally
managed to issue licenses

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:41.610
for the fishermen.

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:44.040
And they've told them
that they're on their own

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:46.560
to regulate themselves.

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:48.270
It turns out that Conapesca,

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:50.460
the agency that the
went to at the beginning

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:54.720
is such a rigid, large government agency

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:59.310
that in order to do things
like issue, to enforce quotas,

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:01.623
they have to perform multiple studies.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:07.950
It's like there's so many
levels of bureaucratic red tape.

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:10.320
The system just isn't designed to protect

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:12.210
this sort of one-off discovery.

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:14.490
It's designed to protect populations

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.710
that we already know exist, right?

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:18.210
There's the fishing licenses.

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:20.410
There are patrols by

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:26.853
people who are essentially wildlife,

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:29.883
I don't wanna say police officers, but,

00:30:30.974 --> 00:30:33.210
there are people, agents
who are there to make sure

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:35.280
that everyone is following the rules.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:37.020
There is that system in place,

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:39.300
but it's not able to be nimble enough

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:43.620
to come in last minute and try
and protect something before,

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:44.790
just as it's been discovered

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:46.743
before it's completely wiped out.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.360
So as more callo is
fished out of this bank

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:54.510
that we saw off the coast of Teacapan,

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:57.480
the divers are having to
actually go deeper and deeper

00:30:57.480 --> 00:31:00.630
to continue to extract callo.

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:02.280
And as they go deeper and deeper,

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:05.700
they're also spending far
longer on the ocean floor

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:07.920
and they're getting the bends.

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:11.853
There are also issues, like I
mentioned with the equipment.

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:16.653
There are pipes or there
are tubes that explode.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:23.120
And what the result is
that seven divers die

00:31:23.460 --> 00:31:28.080
because there is not a hyperbaric
chamber in Teacapan right?

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:30.990
They just have a tiny little clinic.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.390
And the closest hyperbaric chamber,

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:37.650
which is used to treat the
bends, is two hours away.

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.810
And in one instance, Belen,

00:31:39.810 --> 00:31:43.830
one of his divers gets sick
and he calls an ambulance

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:46.740
and they agreed to meet him halfway.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:49.230
So, he gets the diver in his truck,

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:52.980
they race as fast as they
can to the halfway point

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:56.040
of Escuinapa and when he gets there,

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:58.710
the ambulance is waiting
and they hoist him

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:00.420
into the ambulance and he takes off

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:02.670
and he gets to Mazatlan in time.

00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:05.160
He's able to get a hyperbaric
into a hyperbaric chamber

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:06.750
and his life is saved.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:11.253
But there's just so many people
who were not as fortunate.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:14.250
And you know,

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:16.710
I think I can imagine that
there are probably some folks

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:19.260
in the group, perhaps
professionally trained divers,

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:24.260
thinking, 'wow, how stupid were people,

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:27.480
what were they thinking
doing this sort of work?'

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:31.290
And for me it really highlights
the level of desperation,

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:34.530
just how far people were willing to go

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:36.960
in order to have a shot
at a decent living, right?

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:38.820
They're putting everything on the line.

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:40.740
They may not know how to dive properly.

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.570
They may not have a clue that there's

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:45.300
a specific limit to how
long they should be down

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:46.710
on the ocean floor.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:48.960
All they know is that this is their chance

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:52.290
to earn money for themselves and perhaps

00:32:52.290 --> 00:32:53.883
their families as well.

00:32:56.220 --> 00:33:01.050
So at this point, nothing
can stop the onslaught.

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:02.040
People are dying.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:05.010
Belen has tried to entice
the fishermen to fish less

00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:07.440
by giving them better
prices for their catch.

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:10.920
Nothing works, no one
is willing to relent.

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:13.410
And this is when it becomes
a race to the bottom.

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:16.650
Because when you hold back on your catch,

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:19.680
but you know your neighbor
is just gonna go out and fish

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:23.700
that portion anyway, why would you comply?

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:26.790
And eventually even Belen gives up.

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:29.880
He sends out all his boats
and decides that he'll reap

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:32.670
as much benefit as he's able to

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:33.930
because down the line he knows

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.000
that just like everyone else,

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.400
he's gonna be completely screwed.

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:42.240
He might as well enjoy
this moment while he can,

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:45.270
earn what he can before it's gone

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:48.070
because they're all gonna
have to kind of suffer through

00:33:49.976 --> 00:33:54.480
the reality afterwards of being left

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:55.730
without anything to fish.

00:33:57.737 --> 00:33:59.820
And so we're now finally arriving

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:02.163
at the end of the story of callo de hacha.

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:05.190
Nearly a year and a half, nearly sorry,

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:08.070
less than a year since
they were discovered.

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.640
Now there are so many
shells and callo guts

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:14.100
that the fishermen have
been carelessly tossing

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:19.100
over their boats as they
harvest and clean the callo

00:34:19.140 --> 00:34:21.120
that that's polluted the water.

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:23.160
And it's ruined this ecosystem

00:34:23.160 --> 00:34:28.160
that allowed the callo to
thrive down there for decades.

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.960
This causes a mass die off

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:34.680
and the last callo to be
extracted or fished out

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:35.733
are already dead.

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:39.780
So we're at the end of our story.

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:43.580
Today, Teacapan is nearly,
the fishing in Teacapan

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:45.270
is nearly at an end.

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:46.890
Over fishing and climate change

00:34:46.890 --> 00:34:51.120
has really brought the
industry to its knees.

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:53.130
And the young people
don't follow their parents

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:56.220
into this work anymore because
they know it's grueling

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:58.890
and it can't really provide a living.

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:00.930
Most people, including the Belen's kids,

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:03.000
have left the town for work in the city

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:04.983
or even in the United States.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.910
So if my small presentation
has peaked your interest,

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:10.980
I just wanna point you all
towards the full story,

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:12.930
which you can hear on Snap Judgment.

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:14.970
The episode's called Mother Nature.

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.530
And I wanna give a brief shout out

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:18.630
to the Food and Environment
Reporting Network

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:22.530
where I'm a contributing
writer and they fund reporting

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:25.020
that focuses on agriculture
and environment.

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:26.760
And without their support,

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:28.760
this story would not have been possible.

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:34.800
And now I just wanna pivot a
little bit and talk a bit about

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:37.263
what I do as an agricultural journalist.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:43.560
So I have spent the last
few years telling everyone

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:45.750
that I am not a science journalist

00:35:45.750 --> 00:35:49.590
and yet I keep getting invited
to speak at science events.

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:52.440
And I think this comes from
a place of my own insecurity,

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:55.230
mainly because my stories,

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:56.367
and if you listen to them or read them,

00:35:56.367 --> 00:36:01.367
are not often chocked full
of data analytics and graphs.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:03.160
I am horrible at math

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:08.100
and I might not even
directly quote a scientist

00:36:08.100 --> 00:36:10.830
in the work that I do because
I think it's so important

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:13.800
to keep the workers or the producers

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:17.223
at the center of my stories.

00:36:19.638 --> 00:36:22.020
But I'm starting to
notice a sort of shift.

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:24.870
I've been cultivating this
beat for the last several years

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:27.570
and I often say that I
report on the intersection

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.450
of agriculture and immigration.

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:32.280
And we're talking about farm workers,

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:33.510
like you see in this photo,

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:35.220
we're talking about meat packing workers

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:37.170
and fishermen like Belen.

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:41.310
But now I'm starting to think
that I need to change that

00:36:41.310 --> 00:36:45.093
to say the intersection of
agriculture and climate change.

00:36:46.470 --> 00:36:47.700
Now, more than ever,

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:50.490
the impacts of climate change
are increasingly relevant

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:54.090
to my reporting because these
are the people who are living

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:56.283
on the front lines of this crisis.

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:00.210
As the temperatures
rise, not only will this,

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:01.890
will our food be impacted,

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:06.120
but more importantly will impact
the people who produce it.

00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:07.560
So in this photo here,

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:12.030
we have migrant workers
who are harvesting lettuce

00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:13.890
in Yuma, Arizona.

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:17.430
They cross the border every day
from Mexico to do this work.

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:20.580
And in the fall in Yuma,

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:24.240
average temperatures
are around 102 degrees,

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:27.930
which is the start of lettuce season.

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:32.520
And on average, a US farm
worker endures 21 unsafe days

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:33.600
during the growing season.

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:37.020
And by mid-century that's
set to increase to 39 days.

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:39.540
And we are already seeing harrowing news

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:42.420
of farm workers dying from heat stroke

00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:46.800
in places you might not expect
like Washington and Oregon.

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:50.433
So this is very rapidly becoming
one of the top concerns.

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:54.330
And we see this too in
places like Teacapan,

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:56.250
how climate change is impacting them.

00:37:56.250 --> 00:37:58.770
Warming waters mean
that the schools of fish

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:01.920
that these fishermen rely
on are seeking cooler waters

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:03.420
out in the open ocean.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:05.040
So you'll remember I
showed you those photos

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:06.570
of folks coming back in the morning

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:09.960
from having spent the
the night out fishing.

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:13.020
So this means that the fishermen
must now travel further

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:14.850
in order to catch their fish.

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.790
And that costs them more
in gasoline to get there.

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:20.160
It also means that their work

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:22.530
is becoming increasingly dangerous.

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:25.380
They're out there at night
because schools of fish

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:27.453
tend to be more active under moonlight.

00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:31.560
And yeah, this is all just combined

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.000
to make their job more expensive,

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:35.373
more dangerous, more grueling.

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:41.450
And even if we zoom back to an even larger

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:44.910
and take a more macro
perspective on this intersection

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:47.130
of climate change and agriculture.

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:49.380
I just wanna point out
that the many people

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:52.560
who have gathered in migrant camps

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:54.150
along the US Mexico border,

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:57.990
folks who are hoping to seek
asylum in the United States,

00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:00.840
many of these people are former farmers.

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:03.930
They were driven from their
lands due to climate change,

00:39:03.930 --> 00:39:07.650
due to underproduction of their crops

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:12.210
due to unreliable rains
and weather patterns.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:14.340
And they moved to cities and in the cities

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:16.140
they were vulnerable to
things like extortion

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:17.700
and gang violence.

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:21.810
And so from there sought refuge north.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:26.400
So it's all very, very interconnected.

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:27.870
I think a lot of times when I tell people

00:39:27.870 --> 00:39:29.190
that I report on agriculture,

00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:32.680
I get a lot of blank
stares and I admit that

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.520
even to me it sounds a bit boring.

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:37.470
But when we start to see our world

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:39.750
through this perspective of food systems

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:41.160
and who produces our food,

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:42.783
I think it's really fascinating.

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:46.860
And it can tell you so
much about our politics,

00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:48.870
our own sense of empathy,

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:51.663
and even our understanding of science.

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:55.770
And in our current generation,

00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:58.380
I think this is going to become
one of the most important

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:03.380
topics of our time, our farm workers

00:40:03.570 --> 00:40:04.410
and climate change,

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:06.213
food system and climate change.

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:15.770
I'm gonna just leave my
presentation real quick

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:19.900
'cause I wanna make sure
we have time for a video.

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:24.990
Belen was not able to join us here.

00:40:24.990 --> 00:40:28.080
He does not have a very
reliable internet connection.

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:30.450
This is also the day that
his sons are coming back

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:32.650
from the US after a very long stint

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:35.760
working on a farm in Washington.

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:37.080
They've been there for nine months.

00:40:37.080 --> 00:40:38.640
But I did wanna be able to include him

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:40.353
in this presentation somehow.

00:40:41.580 --> 00:40:45.150
So the folks at the Franke
program were very generous

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:47.550
and helped me to put
subtitles to a quick interview

00:40:47.550 --> 00:40:49.287
that I did with him via video.

00:40:49.287 --> 00:40:54.060
And I've asked him what does
he hope comes from this story

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:56.940
being published and out there
and what does he believe

00:40:56.940 --> 00:41:00.597
that the fisherman have learned
from the callo de hacha.

00:41:01.537 --> 00:41:04.454
(speaking Spanish)

00:41:13.964 --> 00:41:16.881
(speaking Spanish)

00:41:26.024 --> 00:41:28.941
(speaking Spanish)

00:41:41.471 --> 00:41:44.388
(speaking Spanish)

00:41:57.189 --> 00:42:00.106
(speaking Spanish)

00:42:18.046 --> 00:42:20.963
(speaking Spanish)

00:42:35.669 --> 00:42:38.586
(speaking Spanish)

00:42:54.318 --> 00:42:57.235
(speaking Spanish)

00:43:09.167 --> 00:43:12.084
(speaking Spanish)

00:43:26.396 --> 00:43:29.313
(speaking Spanish)

00:43:44.716 --> 00:43:49.346
- But I just wanna finish up
with pointing out some of the,

00:43:49.346 --> 00:43:50.940
a little bit of the different
style that I work in,

00:43:50.940 --> 00:43:54.030
which is narrative storytelling
and how that can overlap

00:43:54.030 --> 00:43:56.640
with science to make it more powerful.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:59.730
So you, listening to my story,

00:43:59.730 --> 00:44:01.320
if you have the opportunity might notice

00:44:01.320 --> 00:44:03.480
there's not a whole lot of science there

00:44:03.480 --> 00:44:06.900
in terms of numbers and terminology.

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:10.080
And I worked with a
really brilliant editor,

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:11.310
her name is Nancy Lopez,

00:44:11.310 --> 00:44:12.870
and she really worked
with me to kind of trim

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:15.060
all of that out because she, like I,

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:18.033
really believe in the
power of a good story.

00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:24.600
Unlike a traditional news article,

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:27.120
these stories have
beginning middles and ends.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:28.500
They have a narrative arc.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.410
They have a character we
follow on their journey

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:34.830
and maybe they can overcome
the challenges they face.

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:38.310
Maybe they can slay the
dragon or maybe they can't,

00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:40.980
maybe they can't stop all of the fishermen

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:44.040
from extracting this natural resource

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:46.050
before it's completely wiped out.

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:49.020
But the idea is that we
follow them on their journey

00:44:49.020 --> 00:44:51.330
and we learn to empathize with them.

00:44:51.330 --> 00:44:52.740
And I think it's such an important tool,

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:55.290
especially when we're
talking about climate change.

00:44:55.290 --> 00:44:58.110
We've been so good at ignoring this crisis

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:00.960
for so many generations,
even with the science

00:45:00.960 --> 00:45:03.510
really being right in front of our faces.

00:45:03.510 --> 00:45:07.410
And I think it's because
it's a huge concept.

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:09.180
I think it's overwhelming.

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:10.680
And I think especially right now,

00:45:10.680 --> 00:45:13.050
we find ourselves in these news cycles

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:15.450
that can be incredibly exhausting, right?

00:45:15.450 --> 00:45:17.700
We open up the news in the morning

00:45:17.700 --> 00:45:19.080
and there's so many stories

00:45:19.080 --> 00:45:21.000
that immediately deserve our attention.

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:23.940
And I'm talking about the pandemic,

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:27.870
I'm talking about
democracy in this country,

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:30.510
a war raging in Europe.

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:32.660
There's so much to pay
attention to right now

00:45:32.660 --> 00:45:34.110
in this age of information.

00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:37.320
And so we really have to
work at how we can go about

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:41.430
getting this information, this notion

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:44.770
or getting people to care, to engage

00:45:45.630 --> 00:45:48.510
when we are also overwhelmed

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:51.000
by the amount of information.

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:53.880
So I really look at a good story

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:57.210
as sort of this Trojan horse, if you will,

00:45:57.210 --> 00:46:01.200
that we can tell a story
that is entertaining,

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:02.880
that is engaging,

00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:06.180
that helps someone empathize with a person

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:07.770
on the other side of the world

00:46:07.770 --> 00:46:12.770
in a completely different
socioeconomic status

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:15.120
and be able to empathize with them,

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:18.100
to see what it's like
to walk in their shoes

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:23.130
and to see what the impacts
of things like climate change,

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:25.590
or in this case overfishing,

00:46:25.590 --> 00:46:29.193
really does to an individual
or to a community.

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:35.130
And so, when we, I think
we've learned to think about

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:38.100
these things separately
for a very long time.

00:46:38.100 --> 00:46:38.933
As I mentioned,

00:46:38.933 --> 00:46:42.390
I'm hesitant to say that
I'm a science journalist

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:45.060
or that my work is scientific in nature.

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:49.380
But when we start to
think about how science

00:46:49.380 --> 00:46:53.250
and good storytelling
can be paired together,

00:46:53.250 --> 00:46:55.260
I think we have the opportunity

00:46:55.260 --> 00:46:59.703
to do something incredibly
powerful and immensely beautiful.

00:47:01.350 --> 00:47:04.080
How can we tell these important stories

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:07.170
in a way that catches the
attention of audiences

00:47:07.170 --> 00:47:08.700
that might not be able to,

00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:10.803
might not want to listen otherwise?

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:15.780
I was just recently reminded
of this this morning,

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:17.400
there was actually some research from Yale

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:20.130
that was published talking about how folks

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:24.120
who are Republican have,

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:29.010
folks who identify as Republican
have a higher rate of death

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:31.560
from the COVID-19 disease.

00:47:31.560 --> 00:47:36.560
And so you have to think, the
science is right there, right?

00:47:36.900 --> 00:47:39.930
We all have seen the
science around COVID-19

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:42.640
and unless there is sometimes

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:47.370
there has to be a good
story to accompany that.

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:50.460
And some people there might be a bad story

00:47:50.460 --> 00:47:55.460
that is telling people the wrong thing.

00:47:55.980 --> 00:47:58.920
So, it's how do we
embrace this information?

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:02.370
How do we tell it through
a story that can do good

00:48:02.370 --> 00:48:05.310
in this world who can get
people again to listen,

00:48:05.310 --> 00:48:06.910
to get on board, to engage

00:48:08.010 --> 00:48:10.960
as opposed to gloss over and as opposed to

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:17.463
take a story in a direction
that it shouldn't.

00:48:19.680 --> 00:48:21.213
So I'm gonna stop sharing.

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:24.990
- Yeah, thank you so much Esther,

00:48:24.990 --> 00:48:28.800
for such a fantastic talk

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:33.300
and explaining to us the multifaceted

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:35.640
and complex human interactions

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:40.283
and the sort of this
interweaving of nature

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:44.250
and human nature and the
decisions that we make

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:47.070
and the complexity of that process.

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:48.540
And you know,

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:51.573
science doesn't have
to be just about data.

00:48:53.201 --> 00:48:57.570
Science really does, is done
in the larger human context.

00:48:57.570 --> 00:49:01.050
And so I, I don't know,

00:49:01.050 --> 00:49:04.620
I consider your story
to be deeply intertwined

00:49:04.620 --> 00:49:08.550
with scientific concerns
and scientific questions.

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:12.030
So let me throw open
the floor for questions.

00:49:12.030 --> 00:49:16.020
I don't see anything in the Q&amp;A yet,

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:20.043
so please feel free to add
your questions in there.

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:24.630
- Thank you so much for
this entirely engrossing

00:49:24.630 --> 00:49:27.090
and very tragic story.

00:49:27.090 --> 00:49:29.820
And for telling it and bringing
this all to our attention

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:31.113
and a wider audience.

00:49:32.610 --> 00:49:35.310
Ever the optimist, what are the prospects

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:38.760
for the callo coming back in this area

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:42.600
and what are Belen's thoughts
about what he, if he's around,

00:49:42.600 --> 00:49:46.290
what he might do next time
or not do in that situation?

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:49.110
- Yeah, Belen is a big dreamer and he is,

00:49:49.110 --> 00:49:52.710
I think after I talked
to his family about,

00:49:52.710 --> 00:49:54.870
just asking some questions
about his character,

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:57.750
it turns out that he is ever the champion

00:49:57.750 --> 00:49:59.040
of sustainable fishing.

00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:02.280
And it comes to anything
from shrimp to sea bass.

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:04.470
He is there trying to mitigate issues

00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:09.030
and ensure that everyone is
respecting this wildlife.

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.490
That's, I think, something very
near and dear to his heart.

00:50:11.490 --> 00:50:14.670
And so, it's been 15 years,

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:17.410
since that callo bank was nearly wiped out

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:23.040
and it had probably sat
undisturbed for decades.

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:24.780
I don't know how long the estimates,

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:26.490
I'm not sure what they look like,

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:29.280
but who knows how long it
will take to grow back.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:31.830
The fact is that it likely
will or that it already has

00:50:31.830 --> 00:50:36.090
started to grow back and it's
sort of like poking the bear.

00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:38.430
When do we make the decision
to get a diver out here

00:50:38.430 --> 00:50:39.420
to bring them back down

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:42.300
to see what it looks like down there now.

00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:45.450
Belen is very eager to do this.

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:48.480
He, I think because I took
an interest in this story,

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:50.670
really wants to explore it with me.

00:50:50.670 --> 00:50:52.380
He wants to get a diver to come down.

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:54.280
He wants to see what's down there now

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:59.340
even though there have not
been any changes necessarily

00:50:59.340 --> 00:51:02.460
to change what the
outcome would look like,

00:51:02.460 --> 00:51:05.460
he believes that fishermen,
then the town have learned.

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:08.610
They learned their lesson,
they would never do that again.

00:51:08.610 --> 00:51:10.830
And perhaps talking to him,

00:51:10.830 --> 00:51:12.360
I really got the sense
that a lot of people

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:14.400
feel a lot of shame for what happened,

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:19.140
that they didn't act in time,
they didn't act appropriately.

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:21.660
And I think more than anything,

00:51:21.660 --> 00:51:24.780
the death of those seven
divers really weighs on them.

00:51:24.780 --> 00:51:29.780
And so there is an eagerness
to start it all again.

00:51:30.180 --> 00:51:31.830
But there is also this belief

00:51:31.830 --> 00:51:36.150
that perhaps they would be
able to regulate it themselves

00:51:36.150 --> 00:51:39.120
and Belen is also very
much interested in the idea

00:51:39.120 --> 00:51:43.290
of making it into some
sort of a farming system

00:51:43.290 --> 00:51:45.120
so that these could be generated in a way

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:47.310
that I guess oysters are

00:51:47.310 --> 00:51:52.310
to have always accessible
callo in cages in the ocean.

00:51:54.600 --> 00:51:58.893
Something like that, that would
make this more long lasting.

00:52:01.410 --> 00:52:03.450
- Thank you. We join him in his dream.

00:52:03.450 --> 00:52:05.200
You can convey that to him, please.

00:52:06.690 --> 00:52:08.970
Warm regards from Yale.

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:09.900
So from the audience,

00:52:09.900 --> 00:52:11.820
thank you so much for this amazing talk.

00:52:11.820 --> 00:52:12.960
I have a couple questions.

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:16.020
First, how did you come across this story?

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:19.290
And second, what advice do you
have for aspiring journalists

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:21.333
who want to do this kind of work?

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:26.223
- So I think I can
answer that in one swoop.

00:52:27.804 --> 00:52:29.970
I have a relationship with
this town that dates back

00:52:29.970 --> 00:52:31.470
to when I was in high school.

00:52:31.470 --> 00:52:33.630
I was at a public high school,

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:36.060
one that really valued travel.

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:37.410
So instead of sports teams,

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:40.470
we did things like
language immersion trips.

00:52:40.470 --> 00:52:44.010
And we had an exchange set up
with the school at Teacapan.

00:52:44.010 --> 00:52:46.290
So I went down there when I was first 16,

00:52:46.290 --> 00:52:48.000
I stayed with a host family.

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:51.660
I returned again my senior
year of high school.

00:52:51.660 --> 00:52:53.880
And then just thanks to social media,

00:52:53.880 --> 00:52:55.710
my host siblings were my age

00:52:55.710 --> 00:52:57.510
and we continued to stay in touch.

00:52:57.510 --> 00:53:00.330
I spent plenty of time in
Mexico studying Spanish.

00:53:00.330 --> 00:53:03.813
So we were always able to
communicate pretty fluently.

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:07.500
And so that's how I
stumbled upon that story.

00:53:07.500 --> 00:53:11.490
I was actually, that
story I actually heard

00:53:11.490 --> 00:53:13.890
for the first time when
I was in high school.

00:53:13.890 --> 00:53:16.470
And it was something that
just seemed so mystic

00:53:16.470 --> 00:53:21.470
and so unbelievable that one day

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:25.830
just last year it occurred to
me that I could make a story

00:53:25.830 --> 00:53:26.663
out of that.

00:53:26.663 --> 00:53:29.460
That I had the resources,
that I now had the skills.

00:53:29.460 --> 00:53:32.130
And of course I had those connections.

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:37.050
And I think for folks that
aspire to do this sort of work,

00:53:37.050 --> 00:53:39.930
I mean obviously building
that skill set is important,

00:53:39.930 --> 00:53:42.540
but the most important
thing is that innate sense

00:53:42.540 --> 00:53:45.780
of curiosity and looking for
those stories in your own life,

00:53:45.780 --> 00:53:49.290
Like what paths have
you crossed in your life

00:53:49.290 --> 00:53:51.240
with What sorts of people?

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:54.240
What unbelievable stories do
you have in your back pocket

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:56.340
that you might tell friends and family

00:53:56.340 --> 00:53:58.050
when you're all sitting down at dinner

00:53:58.050 --> 00:54:02.820
or when you really wanna impress
someone with a good story.

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:04.203
What do you go back to?

00:54:05.400 --> 00:54:08.190
What stories are already
a part of your life

00:54:08.190 --> 00:54:11.660
that could be made into
something like a story,

00:54:11.660 --> 00:54:12.870
an audio documentary.

00:54:12.870 --> 00:54:15.990
And I think that a lot of us
have more of those stories

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:17.043
than we realize.

00:54:20.580 --> 00:54:24.840
- So Esther, if I may use my prerogative

00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:26.460
to ask you this question.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:29.910
So Sinaloa is a complicated place, right?

00:54:29.910 --> 00:54:31.230
Because isn't that the name

00:54:31.230 --> 00:54:35.640
of one of the big drug cartels in Mexico?

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:36.473
- Yeah.

00:54:36.473 --> 00:54:39.990
- I'm curious about how the larger culture

00:54:39.990 --> 00:54:42.750
of that place kind of steeped with that.

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:46.923
How do you think that
impacted the thinking,

00:54:48.454 --> 00:54:53.043
the way in which they
viewed commercialization?

00:54:56.064 --> 00:54:59.100
- Yeah. Yeah, I can speak to some of that.

00:54:59.100 --> 00:55:02.070
This was 15 years ago when
the callo were extracted,

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:05.910
so the situation was
different, absolutely.

00:55:05.910 --> 00:55:10.350
Now Sinaloa is certainly
a state that's considered

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:12.933
very infiltrated and
controlled by the cartel.

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:16.620
And I would say that
absolutely extends to Teacapan

00:55:16.620 --> 00:55:17.820
to an extent.

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:19.650
So you have things,

00:55:19.650 --> 00:55:23.280
the sale of beer is controlled
by the cartel in that town.

00:55:23.280 --> 00:55:26.970
At 8:00, the stores that sell
beer must close their doors.

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:30.000
And then the only stores
that are open that sell beer

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:31.710
are owned by the cartel.

00:55:31.710 --> 00:55:33.513
So it's pervasive.

00:55:35.400 --> 00:55:36.960
The folks part of this organization,

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:40.200
folks who are part of this
organized, criminal organization

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:45.200
are looking for any way to generate money.

00:55:45.510 --> 00:55:50.010
- Incomes, multiple streams of money and..

00:55:50.010 --> 00:55:51.180
- Exactly.

00:55:51.180 --> 00:55:54.120
- That sort of ethics are not really

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:57.150
part of that discussion there.

00:55:57.150 --> 00:55:59.370
- Right. So what's difficult, I think,

00:55:59.370 --> 00:56:00.930
is that you're looking at a town

00:56:00.930 --> 00:56:03.600
which subsisted for so long off of fishing

00:56:03.600 --> 00:56:06.060
and now that source of
revenue for these families

00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:08.160
is drying up and it's becoming harder.

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:11.010
So your kids either leave
to go somewhere else

00:56:11.010 --> 00:56:14.190
or you're, unfortunately there.

00:56:14.190 --> 00:56:19.190
You face the possibility
of working to some extent

00:56:19.380 --> 00:56:21.510
with these organized gangs.

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:25.020
So I've heard things like
people using their boats

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:27.810
to transport drugs, really dangerous,

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:29.820
very dangerous stuff.

00:56:29.820 --> 00:56:34.020
Even things like cartel
maybe fronting the money

00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:37.620
for gasoline and taking
a cut of the profits

00:56:37.620 --> 00:56:38.820
that you earn from fishing.

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:41.550
And so this highlights something
that's really important,

00:56:41.550 --> 00:56:45.300
which is that not only is this
area already very unstable

00:56:45.300 --> 00:56:48.330
politically in terms of
who controls this state,

00:56:48.330 --> 00:56:50.610
it's very apparent that it is the cartels

00:56:50.610 --> 00:56:54.573
and not necessarily the
government of Mexico.

00:56:55.710 --> 00:56:57.390
But as things become more unstable

00:56:57.390 --> 00:56:59.430
because of climate change,

00:56:59.430 --> 00:57:02.324
people don't have a lot of other options.

00:57:02.324 --> 00:57:05.670
For making a living
and I think we'll start

00:57:05.670 --> 00:57:08.670
to see an increase in instability.

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:11.400
- Yeah, and I think you
made the point wonderfully

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:14.430
that finding sustainable options

00:57:14.430 --> 00:57:16.803
is really, really challenging.

00:57:18.200 --> 00:57:21.810
- Yeah, and Belen is a huge champion

00:57:21.810 --> 00:57:24.810
of there being more shrimp farms,

00:57:24.810 --> 00:57:27.180
there being packaging houses,

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:29.070
things that'll allow them to export,

00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:31.440
to sell to markets directly.

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:33.210
All of this stuff is infrastructure

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:34.710
that if the fishermen had,

00:57:34.710 --> 00:57:37.620
would allow them to possibly
sustain their way of life

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:38.820
to some degree.

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:41.430
There just isn't the upfront investment.

00:57:41.430 --> 00:57:44.160
- Right, and also be
sort of more economically

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:46.890
self-sufficient in a way
and be more in control

00:57:46.890 --> 00:57:47.723
of their destiny, right?

00:57:47.723 --> 00:57:49.200
I mean, I think that's their dream.

00:57:49.200 --> 00:57:50.502
That's what he's dreaming.

00:57:50.502 --> 00:57:52.200
- Right, that's what they had,

00:57:52.200 --> 00:57:54.120
they had that and then it was taken away.

00:57:54.120 --> 00:57:55.140
A lot of that overfishing

00:57:55.140 --> 00:57:59.250
comes from commercial fishing
fleets from foreign countries.

00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:02.820
And of course we know
that low income countries

00:58:02.820 --> 00:58:05.160
have not contributed to
climate change to the extent

00:58:05.160 --> 00:58:06.693
that say America has.

00:58:07.590 --> 00:58:09.480
- Yeah, I was just gonna
bring that point up

00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:12.480
that I think your story is
very emblematic of the point

00:58:12.480 --> 00:58:16.830
that the regions that are going
to actually feel the impact

00:58:16.830 --> 00:58:18.780
of climate change, either directly

00:58:18.780 --> 00:58:21.570
because of floods and
other natural disasters

00:58:21.570 --> 00:58:25.230
or a shrinking of their
old ways of livelihood,

00:58:25.230 --> 00:58:28.920
are sadly not the places
that have contributed

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:32.550
in a major way to the carbon

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:36.900
and to climate change and
the deleterious effects

00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:38.070
of climate change, yeah.

00:58:38.070 --> 00:58:41.850
So I really found that your
story was really beautiful

00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:43.410
at sort of multiple levels.

00:58:43.410 --> 00:58:48.410
So thank you once again for
this gorgeous study and book.

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:53.313
So we have a question from
Sylvia Peck in the audience.

00:58:54.840 --> 00:58:57.991
Oh, sorry, I missed one
question prior to that.

00:58:57.991 --> 00:59:00.450
The question is from one
of someone in the audience

00:59:00.450 --> 00:59:03.240
who asked, I know you worked
in radio journalism for a while

00:59:03.240 --> 00:59:04.290
earlier in your career.

00:59:04.290 --> 00:59:06.330
How did you pivot to becoming a freelancer

00:59:06.330 --> 00:59:08.730
and writing about agriculture?

00:59:08.730 --> 00:59:11.520
- I actually began
writing about agriculture,

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:13.710
it was my beat as my last job

00:59:13.710 --> 00:59:16.080
at a public radio station for NPR.

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:18.420
I was part of a regional collaborative

00:59:18.420 --> 00:59:20.940
that contributed stories
across the country

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:23.220
to NPR about agriculture.

00:59:23.220 --> 00:59:25.770
And I just found that I really loved it

00:59:25.770 --> 00:59:26.940
and I really wanted to focus

00:59:26.940 --> 00:59:28.830
on labor in agriculture specifically.

00:59:28.830 --> 00:59:31.470
That had been a passion
of mine for a long time.

00:59:31.470 --> 00:59:35.340
And what I found was that
I just got a little tired

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:39.180
of the NPR format as much
as I loved working for them,

00:59:39.180 --> 00:59:41.190
I really wanted to be
able to do more long form

00:59:41.190 --> 00:59:42.390
in depth stuff.

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:45.420
Things that really allow
characters to evolve

00:59:45.420 --> 00:59:49.290
and allow for a bit more
flexibility and creativity.

00:59:49.290 --> 00:59:53.190
And so I made the decision
to go freelance in 2019,

00:59:53.190 --> 00:59:55.080
just a few months before the pandemic,

00:59:55.080 --> 00:59:58.620
I quit my job and I moved to Mexico

00:59:58.620 --> 01:00:01.230
and it had been a long
held dream of mine, right?

01:00:01.230 --> 01:00:03.540
To do international work especially.

01:00:03.540 --> 01:00:06.720
And so when it seemed that
the world was literally

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:07.830
just caving in on me,

01:00:07.830 --> 01:00:11.070
'cause I had made this
risk during such really,

01:00:11.070 --> 01:00:12.180
just bad timing.

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:15.930
It wound up actually
being fairly advantageous

01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:19.410
because suddenly the topic

01:00:19.410 --> 01:00:22.620
that I had now spent
three years cultivating,

01:00:22.620 --> 01:00:27.620
this beat, was suddenly
on headlines nationally.

01:00:27.870 --> 01:00:30.390
It became an immensely important issue

01:00:30.390 --> 01:00:32.520
because we were,

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:35.010
for the first time a lot of
Americans were being forced

01:00:35.010 --> 01:00:36.960
to contemplate where
their food comes from.

01:00:36.960 --> 01:00:38.130
And if those people are sick,

01:00:38.130 --> 01:00:40.590
they can't process it,
they can't harvest it.

01:00:40.590 --> 01:00:44.430
And so in a way it was,
and I hate to say this,

01:00:44.430 --> 01:00:49.430
but it was fortunate
that the timing was such.

01:00:49.770 --> 01:00:53.550
And so I think those sorts
of elements have allowed me

01:00:53.550 --> 01:00:57.300
to pick up a fairly
successful freelance career

01:00:57.300 --> 01:00:58.263
from the beginning.

01:01:00.780 --> 01:01:03.540
- Thanks, Esther. So an
insightful comment from Sylvia,

01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:06.990
and we can build on it
and turn into a question.

01:01:06.990 --> 01:01:07.980
She says she wanted to tell you

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:10.530
that she thinks you presented
this story beautifully.

01:01:10.530 --> 01:01:11.730
I have to agree.

01:01:11.730 --> 01:01:14.070
And she noticed that you
avoided making villains,

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:17.940
which in a Cinderella windfall
story might be tempting.

01:01:17.940 --> 01:01:21.150
The benefit of your narrative
left us eager to learn more

01:01:21.150 --> 01:01:22.710
and to feel included.

01:01:22.710 --> 01:01:25.290
So maybe you could speak a bit to that.

01:01:25.290 --> 01:01:28.350
How did you avoid that glaring temptation

01:01:28.350 --> 01:01:32.253
to identify villains and protagonists?

01:01:35.310 --> 01:01:38.400
- I think that honestly it was just trying

01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:40.380
to follow the good story.

01:01:40.380 --> 01:01:43.710
The good story is the hero
and he's the center of it.

01:01:43.710 --> 01:01:45.870
And we're watching him step by step.

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:47.640
I think if there's a
villain in this story,

01:01:47.640 --> 01:01:51.120
it's perhaps the regulatory
bodies of Mexico.

01:01:51.120 --> 01:01:52.530
And as soon as that leaves my mouth,

01:01:52.530 --> 01:01:55.923
I'm like immediately kind
of, it gets really boring.

01:01:57.480 --> 01:01:59.550
And we all know,

01:01:59.550 --> 01:02:01.710
we all know what that's
like for the most part.

01:02:01.710 --> 01:02:03.963
That the regulations aren't working.

01:02:04.890 --> 01:02:07.410
I feel like we can assume that is true,

01:02:07.410 --> 01:02:08.970
not just perhaps in Mexico,

01:02:08.970 --> 01:02:11.310
but other countries around the world.

01:02:11.310 --> 01:02:13.590
And so I didn't really
wanna spend too much time

01:02:13.590 --> 01:02:17.130
diving into that just because
I felt like it took away

01:02:17.130 --> 01:02:21.430
from the story and I didn't wanna

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:27.000
also make this about the other
fishermen who are coming in.

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:28.530
There was a big issue towards the end,

01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:30.780
what Belen called pirate fishermen

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:33.317
that were coming in at night
and they were fishing the callo

01:02:33.317 --> 01:02:36.510
before any of the other
fishermen could get out there.

01:02:36.510 --> 01:02:39.120
These were guys from far away.

01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:44.100
And I also didn't feel like it
was fair to villainize them,

01:02:44.100 --> 01:02:47.403
like just the fishermen
of Teacapan were very,

01:02:49.050 --> 01:02:53.580
they were very adamant that
they weren't gonna stop people

01:02:53.580 --> 01:02:55.530
from coming into their
town and fishing callo

01:02:55.530 --> 01:02:58.440
because everyone is just trying to get by.

01:02:58.440 --> 01:03:00.930
They're all in the struggles
together in a lot of ways.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:04.320
And so if they weren't,

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:06.900
Belen can be angry at the
doctors and the ranchers

01:03:06.900 --> 01:03:08.580
and the politicians who got boats

01:03:08.580 --> 01:03:10.080
and went in and fish callo.

01:03:10.080 --> 01:03:15.080
But I also feel that as a
reporter from the United States,

01:03:15.270 --> 01:03:17.880
I honestly don't know if
their economic standing

01:03:17.880 --> 01:03:20.430
is even much better perhaps than my own

01:03:20.430 --> 01:03:21.810
if they're middle class in Mexico.

01:03:21.810 --> 01:03:23.760
But, you know what I'm saying?

01:03:23.760 --> 01:03:26.520
Like I don't think it's
fair as someone from my,

01:03:26.520 --> 01:03:30.302
someone from my position to
necessarily villainize anyone

01:03:30.302 --> 01:03:31.980
in this situation because I've been born

01:03:31.980 --> 01:03:34.773
with so much opportunity
and relative wealth.

01:03:35.670 --> 01:03:39.213
So I think it was just
checking my privilege honestly.

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:46.020
- Oh, but Esther, it's also
wonderful to hear the humility,

01:03:46.020 --> 01:03:49.110
the place of humility and
learning that you have come from.

01:03:49.110 --> 01:03:51.840
I think that makes the story,

01:03:51.840 --> 01:03:53.883
it shines through in the story.

01:03:55.170 --> 01:03:56.003
- [Esther] Well, thank you.

01:03:56.003 --> 01:03:57.813
- As you can see, we are all gushy fans.

01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:05.040
So are there any further
questions for Esther

01:04:05.040 --> 01:04:06.750
before we close?

01:04:06.750 --> 01:04:09.993
Ty, did you wanna have the final question?

01:04:12.180 --> 01:04:13.480
- Where's your next story?

01:04:14.445 --> 01:04:16.395
Not in Mexico. What are you working on?

01:04:17.443 --> 01:04:21.990
- My next dream story is about

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:24.660
these canals in Mexico City,

01:04:24.660 --> 01:04:27.690
that it's an area now called Xochimilco.

01:04:27.690 --> 01:04:31.140
And these canals were what
originally the mechica,

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:35.070
or the indigenous community
that Cortez discovered

01:04:35.070 --> 01:04:37.080
when he discovered Mexico,

01:04:37.080 --> 01:04:39.030
were living then in Mexico City.

01:04:39.030 --> 01:04:40.140
This is the area where they lived

01:04:40.140 --> 01:04:41.640
and they had built these canals

01:04:41.640 --> 01:04:44.040
and their whole city was a floating city.

01:04:44.040 --> 01:04:45.930
Of course that's all been destroyed.

01:04:45.930 --> 01:04:49.080
And so what's left is a
small region of the city

01:04:49.080 --> 01:04:51.930
called Xochimilco where some
of these canals still exist.

01:04:53.670 --> 01:04:58.670
And there is this wild
story from the seventies

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:03.540
when politicians in Mexico
wanted to make Xochimilco

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:06.690
more attractive as a tourist destination.

01:05:06.690 --> 01:05:09.270
And someone had the idea
to introduce manatees

01:05:09.270 --> 01:05:11.100
into the canals.

01:05:11.100 --> 01:05:15.720
And what resulted was that
the manatees did not survive.

01:05:15.720 --> 01:05:17.027
It was not the right environment for them.

01:05:17.027 --> 01:05:19.260
It was far too cold.

01:05:19.260 --> 01:05:21.480
But then rumors started to spread

01:05:21.480 --> 01:05:24.270
that locals were catching
them and eating them

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:27.840
and making manatee tacos.

01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:30.780
And so, I know this sounds all
really wild and preposterous,

01:05:30.780 --> 01:05:33.510
but it was a story that
was so unfathomable

01:05:33.510 --> 01:05:36.630
that when I heard it, I
thought it was just folklore.

01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:38.370
And actually by doing research discovered

01:05:38.370 --> 01:05:40.713
that it did in fact
happen though it is very,

01:05:41.580 --> 01:05:44.250
it's a very sort of like succinct chapter

01:05:44.250 --> 01:05:46.650
in Mexico's history and
there isn't a whole lot

01:05:46.650 --> 01:05:47.703
written about it.

01:05:48.600 --> 01:05:53.190
But it's an example and a really
enthralling story, I think,

01:05:53.190 --> 01:05:57.510
about what happens when humans try to mess

01:05:57.510 --> 01:06:00.090
with a natural environment too much.

01:06:00.090 --> 01:06:02.812
Like you have a perfect
ecosystem, which is

01:06:02.812 --> 01:06:06.120
this swamp, this canal area,

01:06:06.120 --> 01:06:08.520
and you can't just introduce
whatever animal that you want

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:09.810
into it to make it into

01:06:09.810 --> 01:06:14.010
some sort of a lucrative opportunity.

01:06:14.010 --> 01:06:16.080
So, we'll see.

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:17.490
We're still, I'm still working on it.

01:06:17.490 --> 01:06:20.550
There's also plenty of
other stories in the works,

01:06:20.550 --> 01:06:24.300
but I feel like that was more up the,

01:06:24.300 --> 01:06:28.110
sort of in line with the
callo de hacha piece.

01:06:28.110 --> 01:06:30.000
So we'll see how it turns out.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:31.590
And when I can get back to Mexico City

01:06:31.590 --> 01:06:33.060
to do those interviews.

01:06:33.060 --> 01:06:35.190
- I think we'd all agree
there're gonna be anybody better

01:06:35.190 --> 01:06:37.590
to tell that story. Look forward to it.

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:38.423
Bring it to us.

01:06:39.870 --> 01:06:40.800
- Well thank you.

01:06:40.800 --> 01:06:41.700
Thank you all so much

01:06:41.700 --> 01:06:43.920
for having me as a part of this.

01:06:43.920 --> 01:06:45.960
It really was such a great opportunity.

01:06:45.960 --> 01:06:47.040
- Yeah, thank you.

01:06:47.040 --> 01:06:50.430
It was a real privilege
for us to hear from you.

01:06:50.430 --> 01:06:51.630
Thank you so much Esther,

01:06:51.630 --> 01:06:55.290
and here's wishing you the
best in your next adventure

01:06:55.290 --> 01:06:58.628
and we eagerly look forward
to hearing more about it.

01:06:58.628 --> 01:07:01.560
So behalf of Franke
program and the audience,

01:07:01.560 --> 01:07:03.570
thanks so much for coming today.

01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:05.771
- All right, you thank you so much.

01:07:05.771 --> 01:07:06.990
It was a pleasure.

01:07:06.990 --> 01:07:08.907
- Yeah. Bye bye everyone.

01:07:08.907 --> 01:07:09.740
- Bye.

